  .wiki-content .table-scroll > table {
    display: table;
    width: 100%;
    /* table-layout:fixed is what actually constrains these tables. With
       table-layout:auto a percentage width resolves against the table's own
       MIN-CONTENT width, so `width:100%` / `max-width:100%` do NOTHING and the
       table takes its max-content width (measured: 440px inside a 272px
       wrapper at 320px, which pushed the whole PAGE to 359px scrollWidth).
       Fixed layout distributes the 100% across declared columns and lets the
       mobile wrapping rules below do their job. */
    table-layout: fixed;
    max-width: 100%;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* Long unbroken tokens (source path references like
     "Family History/Records/Telfer_James_census_1841-06-06_Castleton_784-13-...")
     cannot wrap and blow out the page width on a phone. Measured 25 Oct 2026:
     a single <p> was 335px wide in a 272px container at 320px viewport.
     `anywhere` is correct HERE (body prose) — it is only forbidden on table
     CELLS, where it shreds ordinary words (see the mobile rule below). */
  .wiki-content p,
  .wiki-content li {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Some body text is not wrapped in <p>/<li> — it sits as a direct child of
     .wiki-content (measured 25 Oct 2026: a raw "Family History/Records/
     Stribling_..." path node holding the page at 518px on a 390px screen).
     Cover every block-level text container. */
  .wiki-content,
  .wiki-content div,
  .wiki-content section,
  .wiki-content figure,
  .wiki-content figcaption,
  .wiki-content blockquote {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Wide multi-column tables (5-6 cols, e.g. the Agnes Telfer census table)
     cannot fit a 320px phone at any sane font size. Let the wrapper scroll
     properly instead of letting fixed layout cram cells to unreadable slivers. */
  .wiki-content .table-scroll > table.wide-table {
    table-layout: auto;
    width: max-content;
    min-width: 100%;
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* Mobile: let cells wrap. On desktop there is room and single-line rows read
     better; on a phone a wide column (e.g. Notes) pushes the table past the
     screen width and the content becomes unreachable (no visible scrollbar).
     `display:block` + `overflow-x:auto` above gives the table its own scroll
     container, so wrapping only needs to handle long unbroken tokens.
     The first-column nowrap/floor above is deliberately NOT relaxed here:
     short labels must never split.

     MEASURED FAILURE (20 Oct 2026, 390px iPhone screenshot): "Born/Baptised"
     rendered as "Bor/n/B/apti/sed" and "Baptised 14 Jul 1761, Castleton" as
     "Bap/tise/d 14/Jul/1761/'/Cast/leto/n". Cause: three columns at
     table-layout:auto with `word-break: break-word` + `overflow-wrap: anywhere`
     — the browser satisfies the header's min-content width by hyphen-less
     CRACKING words instead of sizing columns sensibly, because `anywhere`
     removes every word's minimum width. Fix: use `overflow-wrap: break-word`
     (normal min-content, still breaks a genuinely over-long token) and give
     short word-like headers a min-width floor so they wrap on SPACES only.

     Do NOT reintroduce `overflow-wrap: anywhere` on table cells — it looks like
     a harmless safety net and is in fact what shreds these tables on phones. */
  @media (max-width: 639px) {
